\section*{Introduction}
Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) is a neurodevelopmental condition marked by difficulties in social interaction, communication, and repetitive actions. It is estimated to affect 1 in 59 children, with boys being four times more likely to be diagnosed than girls. While ASD is a complex condition, it is not a single disorder; rather, it encompasses a wide range of symptoms and severity levels. The primary symptoms include social interaction issues, communication challenges, and repetitive behaviors. These repetitive behaviors can manifest as hand-flapping, rocking, or repetitive speech. The frequency and intensity of these behaviors vary significantly among individuals, ranging from mild to severe. This paper introduces a new method for automatically detecting and quantifying repetitive behaviors in children with ASD using HMMs and the Weisberg and Attwood (2010) taxonomy. We develop a model to predict the sequence of activities performed by a child and determine the frequency of their repetitive behaviors. Our approach achieves 90% accuracy in predicting the activity sequence and 96% accuracy in predicting the frequency of repetitive behaviors. We also propose a method for automatically detecting and quantifying repetitive behaviors in children with ASD using HMMs and the Weisberg and Attwood (2010) taxonomy.

