\section*{1. Introduction}
Deep learning has achieved remarkable success across numerous domains, including computer vision, natural language processing, and speech recognition. However, the deployment of these models in real-world applications raises significant privacy concerns. For instance, in natural language processing, attackers may attempt to reconstruct training data by leveraging the model's outputs and internal parameters. To address these threats, recent studies have examined privacy issues in deep learning, with a primary focus on image data. In contrast, this paper examines the privacy vulnerabilities of language modeling, specifically targeting the leakage of word-level information. We propose a new privacy metric for NLP to measure this risk and analyze the privacy threats posed by language models. Our results reveal that current language models are susceptible to privacy attacks. Additionally, we assess the privacy and accuracy trade-offs of various defense strategies, such as differential privacy, adversarial training, and data augmentation, using the WikiText-2 dataset. The experiments show that these approaches can enhance privacy protection with only a minimal reduction in accuracy. The code is available at https://github.com/ChenYuan1999/Privacy-in-NLP. The paper can be found at https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.01999.

